_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 12
---
order: 12
---
short_label: II.12
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In obtuse-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the obtuse angle is greater than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the obtuse angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the obtuse angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off outside by the perpendicular towards the obtuse angle.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} be an obtuse-angled triangle having the angle {BAC} obtuse, and draw {BD} from the point {B} perpendicular to {CA} produced. [!just I.12]

I say that the square on {BC} is greater than the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AC} by twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII12.gif" alt="II.12"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure is already the whole argument — the obtuse triangle, CA
    // produced to D, and the perpendicular BD. Nothing is constructed after
    // slide 2; the rest of the proof is algebra on squares the diagram never
    // draws. The canvas is 250x230 with the triangle filling it, so a square
    // on BC (185px a side), or the rectangle CA by AD (100x70, hanging below
    // DC), would land off-canvas. Refs therefore stay LITERAL, as in II.1:
    // {BC} lights the segment BC, and the words "the square on" are carried
    // by the caption.
    //
    // Everything added below is fully transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0
    // polygons), so the static figure is unchanged. The two angle markers are
    // ordinary coloured elements, but showAngles defaults to false (0.9.0+),
    // so they are absent from the resting figure and arrive with the walk —
    // Euclid's diagram draws no angle arcs.
    //
    // Colour: the triangle keeps Joyce's random fill. BDC CONTAINS BDA and
    // ABC (A lies between D and C), so it is left unfilled and its own parts
    // fill it — note the explicit ";0" face, without which a highlighted
    // polygon paints a near-white OPAQUE face over everything beneath it
    // (#140). Markers follow I.47: red = right angle, magenta = the angle the
    // proposition is about.
    var TRI  = ["A","B","C","ABC","AB","BC","AC","angBAC"];
    var PERP = TRI.concat(["D","BD","DA","DC"]);
    var RT1  = PERP.concat(["angD","BDC"]);
    var RT2  = RT1.concat(["BDA"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "II.12",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;100,200",
            "B;point;free;30,30",
            "C;point;free;200,200",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "BD;line;foot;B,A,C",
            "D;point;last;BD",
            // Joyce declared this connect;D,A. Same segment, same name, same
            // ink — but the endpoints are swapped so the straightEdge stroke
            // runs A→D, the direction CA is actually produced in on slide 2.
            "DA;line;connect;A,D",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — the triangle above already
            // draws all three of these sides.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            // The whole cut line CD, which DA and AC divide between them:
            // the II.4 step, and II.1's whole-and-parts idiom.
            "DC;line;connect;D,C;0;0;0",
            // The two right triangles the two I.47 steps are about. BDC is
            // the container (BDA + ABC), so it stays unfilled.
            "BDC;polygon;triangle;B,D,C;0;0;0;0",
            "BDA;polygon;triangle;B,D,A;0;0;0;0",
            // The obtuse angle of the hypothesis, and the right angle at D.
            // angD is built on arms B,C rather than B,A deliberately: A lies
            // on ray DC, so angle BDA = angle BDC and ONE marker serves both
            // I.47 steps. One marker per vertex, so no radius override.
            "angBAC;sector;angleMarker;A,B,C;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angD;sector;angleMarker;D,B,C;0;0;red;red"
        ],
        // No deferDraggables: A, B and C are the only draggables, and slide 1
        // introduces all three as the given triangle. D is the opposite case —
        // a derived point (point;last) is NOT auto-unioned, so it is listed
        // explicitly in every visible set from slide 2 on, or it loses its dot
        // and its label.
        aliases: { "BAC": "angBAC",
                   "CA": "AC", "BA": "AB", "CB": "BC",
                   "DB": "BD", "AD": "DA", "CD": "DC" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABC} be an obtuse-angled triangle having the angle {BAC} obtuse.",
              visible: TRI,
              // angBAC is this slide's animation target, so it is deliberately
              // NOT highlighted (euclid#171). SlateAnimator hides an animated
              // target until its cascade step starts (#83) but sets
              // shouldHighlight at run start, and drawFace renders a hidden
              // element that is highlighted — so listing it here would paint
              // the wedge from t=0 and the sweep would have nothing to reveal.
              // The animation is the emphasis.
              highlighted: ["ABC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "greater than a right angle", ref: "I.Def.11" }] },
            { text: "Draw {BD} from the point {B} perpendicular to {CA} produced.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["AC"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "DA", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "BD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "perpendicular to CA produced", ref: "I.12" }] },
            { text: "I say that the square on {BC} is greater than the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AC} by twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["BC","AB","AC","DA"] },
            { text: "Since the straight line {CD} has been cut at random at the point {A}, the square on {DC} equals the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AD} and twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["DC","AC","DA","A"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the whole DC, cut at A", ref: "II.4" }] },
            // Joyce's two sentences here take a slide each rather than one
            // compressed caption (process.md, "split rather than compress").
            // The first sentence is his verbatim, mangled grammar and all —
            // his page reads "Add the square on DB be added to each."
            { text: "Add the square on {DB} be added to each.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["BD"] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {CD} and {DB} equals the sum of the squares on {CA}, {AD}, and {DB} plus twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["DC","BD","AC","DA"],
              // C.N.2 licenses the resulting equality, so it sits here rather
              // than on the instruction that precedes it.
              justifications: [{ claim: "the square on DB added to each", ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {CB} equals the sum of the squares on {CD} and {DB}, for the angle at {D} is right.",
              visible: RT1,
              highlighted: ["BDC","BC","DC","BD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right triangle BDC", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "And the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB}.",
              visible: RT2,
              // BDA is this slide's animation target — kept out of
              // highlighted, per the note on slide 1.
              highlighted: ["AB","DA","BD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "BDA", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right triangle BDA, at the same right angle", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {CB} equals the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AB} plus twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["BC","AC","AB","DA"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "substitute and take away", ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "So that the square on {CB} is greater than the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AB} by twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["BC","AC","AB","DA","angBAC"] },
            // The closing enunciation, in Joyce's words. His proof names the
            // obtuse angle nowhere after slide 1, so this is the slide whose
            // caption discusses it and where angBAC keeps the gold.
            { text: "Therefore in obtuse-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the obtuse angle is greater than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the obtuse angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the obtuse angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off outside by the perpendicular towards the obtuse angle.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["BC","AC","AB","DA","angBAC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Def.11": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI11/",
        "I.12":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI12/",
        "I.47":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "II.4":     "/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII4/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since the straight line {CD} has been cut at random at the point {A}, the square on {DC} equals the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AD} and twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}. [!just II.4]

Add the square on {DB} be added to each. Therefore the sum of the squares on {CD} and {DB} equals the sum of the squares on {CA}, {AD},  and {DB} plus twice the rectangle {CA} by {AD}.

But the square on {CB} equals the sum of the squares on {CD} and {DB}, for the angle at {D} is right,  and the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB}, therefore the square on {CB} equals the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AB} plus twice the rectangle  {CA} by {AD}, so that the square on {CB} is greater than the sum of the squares on {CA} and {AB} by twice the rectangle  {CA} by {AD}. [!just I.47]

Therefore *in obtuse-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the obtuse angle is greater than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the obtuse angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the obtuse angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off outside by the perpendicular towards the obtuse angle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition for obtuse angles, together with the next one for acute triangles, complement the Pythagorean theorem, @I.47, for right triangles. Prop.I.47 says that if triangle {ABC:canvas_1} has a right angle at {A:canvas_1}, then

where {a:canvas_1}, {b:canvas_1}, and {c:canvas_1} are the sides opposite the angles {A:canvas_1}, {B:canvas_1}, and {C:canvas_1}, respectively.

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="280" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII12a.gif" alt="Oblique triangle"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Guide canvas: commentary, not a proof, so it takes {NAME} hover refs but
    // no slides. This figure IS the II.12 configuration — obtuse at A, foot D
    // outside BA — lettered a, b, c for the law of cosines. Guide refs above
    // this canvas would bind to canvas_0 by the nearest-preceding-canvas rule,
    // so they carry an explicit :canvas_1 selector.
    //
    // One addition: the prose says "the angle A is obtuse rather than right",
    // and that angle had no element. showAngles defaults to false, so the
    // marker never shows in the resting figure; it is reached only by the
    // {A|angA:canvas_1} hover.
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "Oblique triangle",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;100,160",
            "C;point;free;40,40",
            "B;point;free;240,160",
            "a;line;connect;B,C;black",
            "c;line;connect;A,B;black",
            "b;line;connect;A,C;black",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0;random",
            "D;point;foot;C,A,B",
            "h;line;connect;C,D;0;0;gray",
            "DA;line;connect;D,A;0;0;gray",
            "angA;sector;angleMarker;A,C,B;0;0;magenta;magenta"
        ],
        // The guide's formulas name AD; the element is DA.
        aliases: { "AD": "DA" }
    });
</script>
</figure>

<center><span class="elem-ref" data-elem="a" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>a</i></span><sup>2</sup> = <span class="elem-ref" data-elem="b" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>b</i></span><sup>2</sup> + <span class="elem-ref" data-elem="c" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>c</i></span><sup>2</sup></center>

In this proposition, II.12, the angle {A|angA:canvas_1} is obtuse rather than right, and the conclusion is that

<center><span class="elem-ref" data-elem="a" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>a</i></span><sup>2</sup> = <span class="elem-ref" data-elem="b" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>b</i></span><sup>2</sup> + <span class="elem-ref" data-elem="c" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>c</i></span><sup>2</sup> + 2<span class="elem-ref" data-elem="c" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>c</i></span> (<span class="elem-ref" data-elem="AD" data-canvas="canvas_1"><i>AD</i></span>)</center>

The next proposition, @II.13 has a similar conclusion, but the hypothesis is that the angle at {A|angA:canvas_1} is acute rather than obtuse.

#### Law of Cosines

This conclusion is very close to the law of cosines for oblique triangles.

<div class="math display">a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\cos A,</div>

since {AD:canvas_1} equals <span class="math">-b\cos A</span>, the cosine of an obtuse angle being negative. Trigonometry was developed some time after the *Elements* was written, and the negative numbers needed here (for the cosine of an obtuse angle) were not accepted until long after most of trigonometry was developed.  Nonetheless, this proposition and the next may be considered geometric versions of the law of cosines.

Neither this nor the next is used in the rest of the *Elements.*
